								<div class="title" id="post-title-1671788">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1324229"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1356646138 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Dec 2012 22:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1671788">
									

<p>I had considered that to be one possibility while writing. However, unless I'm mistaken, French distinguishes between the second person singular and plural. I imagine that if he were referring to a group of which Maurice was a member, he would say the French equivalent of &quot;y'all,&quot; while if he were referring to Maurice in particular, he would say <em>you</em>. I don't know French grammar at all, so I could be totally off base in this.</p>

							</div>

								
					
